// Emperor Japanese Maple — Acer palmatum 'Emperor I' (Emperor One)
// Edit this file to update plant-specific content.

#import "../../template.typ": *

#plant-header("Emperor Japanese Maple", "Acer palmatum 'Emperor I' (Emperor One)", "japanese_maple")[
  Emperor I is one of the most sun-tolerant Japanese maple cultivars available, featuring striking deep burgundy-red foliage that holds its color through summer heat — unlike many cultivars that fade to green. It leafs out later than most Japanese maples, which helps it avoid late spring freezes. Mature size: 12–15 ft tall, 10 ft wide, slow to moderate growth. Located in the center of the back yard on the west side of the house, this tree receives direct sun from mid-morning onward through the full afternoon — a challenging exposure for this species in Colorado's high-altitude, intense-UV conditions. Water availability is the primary coping mechanism during establishment; adequate moisture before afternoon sun arrives is essential.
]

=== Watering

On drip irrigation with a *2 gph emitter* — the highest output on the property, matched to this tree's position as the most water-demanding drip plant. Japanese maples are shallow-rooted and feel drought stress quickly; they cannot draw on deep moisture reserves the way more established trees can. See the Irrigation & Watering section for the shared drip schedule.

If possible, configure the drip zone serving this tree to run in the morning — the root zone should be maximally hydrated before afternoon sun arrives, more so than for any other plant.

During extended heat waves in July and August (3+ consecutive days above 95°F), the shared schedule at 2 gph may not fully meet this tree's needs given its full-afternoon-sun location. Supplemental hand watering at the drip line — a slow soak with a hose — is appropriate in these conditions. This is a documented system constraint; see the Irrigation & Watering section. Signs of water stress: leaf margins turn brown and crispy, leaves cup downward, or premature leaf drop in summer.

=== Fertilization

Emperor I prefers slightly acidic soil (pH 5.5–6.5), so this location has meaningful pH mismatch and heat compounding risk. Keep the no-phosphorus rule strict.

Use spring ammonium sulfate (21-0-0) as the acidifying N source, then apply the May chelated micronutrient blend plus EDDHA iron in May and again in early July. Keep annual acidic mulch in place and avoid late-season fertilization.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Japanese maples rarely need pruning and are often harmed by over-pruning. Prune in late winter (February) or in summer (July–August) when cuts heal fastest. Focus only on removing dead wood, crossing branches, or branches rubbing the structure. Do not remove more than 1/4 of the canopy in a single season. Always use sharp, sterilized tools.

=== Wildlife Value

Emperor Japanese Maple provides modest direct wildlife value but contributes to garden diversity. Butterflies and native bees occasionally visit the inconspicuous spring flowers. The layered, low branching structure provides cover and shelter for house finches and other small birds. Cottontail rabbits may chew the bark of young or stressed trunks, especially in winter — protect with a hardware cloth cylinder (18" tall) from November through March. Field mice can also gnaw bark at the base; keep mulch several inches from the trunk at all times.

=== Pests & Disease

#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=japanese+maple+leaf+scorch+treatment")[Leaf scorch] — brown, crispy leaf margins — is the most significant issue in this location and is driven by afternoon sun combined with inadequate moisture. It is not a pathogen; address by increasing watering and providing shade relief (see Other Care). #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=japanese+maple+verticillium+wilt")[Verticillium wilt] causes sudden wilting or death of individual branches — prune affected wood 12" below symptoms with sterilized tools; no systemic cure. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=japanese+maple+aphids")[Aphids] may appear on new growth in spring — treat with insecticidal soap. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=japanese+maple+scale+insects+treatment")[Scale insects] can colonize stressed bark; treat with horticultural oil in early spring before leaf-out. A healthy, well-watered tree resists most pest problems.

=== Other Care

MULCHING: Mulch at 4-inch depth (deeper than standard) from 4–6 inches off the trunk out to the full drip line — be generous with the outer diameter. Soil temperature moderation and moisture retention are more critical for this tree than for any other in-ground plant given its exposure. Refresh to 4 inches each spring.

SHADE CLOTH: Install 30–40% shade cloth on the west side of the tree on stakes or a simple frame to filter the most intense afternoon sun from approximately 2pm onward. Install proactively before forecast heat waves — do not wait for scorch to appear. This is especially important during the current establishment years (2025 and 2026 in particular). Wilt-Pruf or similar anti-transpirant spray applied to foliage in early June and again in July also helps by reducing moisture loss through leaves during peak heat.

#info-callout("Long-Term Outlook")[
  This is a second-season establishment plant in a genuinely challenging location — mid-morning through full afternoon sun in Commerce City is near the upper limit of what Emperor I can handle. The tree will always require more attention here than it would in a sheltered east or north-facing spot. As the root system expands over the next 3–4 years, it will progressively become more capable of managing the exposure on its own, and the level of intervention needed will decrease. Emperor I is one of the better Japanese Maple varieties for enduring Front Range establishment conditions. If after 3–4 years of appropriate care — consistent high-output drip, supplemental hand watering in heat waves, shade cloth, mulch maintained to drip line — significant scorch and stress persists through each summer, transplanting to a more sheltered spot in autumn is worth considering. That judgment is premature now.
]
